nullable(),scene:V().trim().min(1).max(2e3),typeInImage:V().trim().max(300),promptFragment:V().trim().min(1).max(4e3),backText:V().trim().min(1).max(400)});B({lookId:V().trim().min(1).max(80),brief:eo,packshotImageUrl:V().url().nullable(),concepts:H(to).min(1)});var no={id:`techno-romanticism`,title:`Techno Romanticism`,description:`1971 print culture: souvenir postcards, riso and offset, tender and analog.`,styleBlock:[`Mid-century Indian print culture, roughly 1940 to 1971: calendar art given away by mills and general stores, hand-painted film posters, matchbox and firework labels, railway and tourism posters, school charts, mill and cooperative advertising.`,`Every image is a printed card that has been scanned: offset halftone rosette across the whole frame, visible paper grain and fibre, faded ink density, warm yellowed cast, lifted milky blacks, low saturation, cream highlights rather than pure white, 0.5mm to 0.8mm colour registration offset on one plate, corner foxing and edge wear.`,`Period photography where photography appears: flat hard frontal or available light, deep focus with everything equally sharp, no shallow depth of field, no bokeh, slight barrel distortion, soft corner vignette.`,`Constant palette: oxidised teal #2E6E6B, warm bone #F2E9D8, ink black #1A1614. The brand's own colour is the single accent and appears sparingly.`,`Emotional register is tender and analog. Never cyberpunk, never chrome, never neon.`].join(`\n`),negatives:[`digital sharpness`,`clean modern colour`,`gradients`,`chrome sheen`,`neon or glow`,`drop shadows`,`glossy 3D render look`,`modern sans-serif typography`,`a redrawn, re-lettered or period-restyled brand logo`,`certification badges, seals or marketplace roundels`,`any text beyond what the concept explicitly specifies`,`invented signage, shop names, slogans or captions anywhere in the scene`].join(`, `),printIntegration:[`The packaging is printed on the same sheet as everything else in the image, not photographed and pasted onto it.`,`It carries the identical halftone screen, ink density, fade, paper grain and registration offset as the rest of the card. Its colours sit inside the card's palette at the same reduced saturation, printed in period ink rather than modern packaging colour.`,`Its edges receive the same slight ink spread and softness as every other printed edge. Nothing about it is sharper, cleaner, brighter or more contrasty than its surroundings.`,`Keep its form, closure, label layout and lettering exactly as the reference. The brand's logo in particular is reproduced exactly as drawn in the reference, with the same letterforms, spelling, spacing, symbol and placement. Never redraw, re-letter, restyle or reposition it, and never let the print treatment dissolve it into illegibility. Only the colour temperature, saturation, sharpness and print texture change, so it belongs to the sheet.`,`Proportions are fixed. Reproduce the reference packaging at its exact aspect ratio and relative dimensions. Never stretch, elongate, squash, taper, widen or otherwise reshape it, and never resize the label panel relative to the pack.`,`Real-world scale must read correctly. The packaging is the physical size it actually is, judged against any hand, person, table, shelf or prop sharing the frame, and a viewer should never think it looks oversized or undersized for what it is. The only exception is when the concept explicitly calls for deliberate scale exaggeration, such as a monument or roadside landmark card, in which case the exaggeration is total and obvious rather than a slightly wrong size.`].join(` `),briefInstruction:[`Read the product and answer four questions. The answers must come from what this product actually is and does, never from the style words.`,`1. The physical action: what a person literally does with it, and when.`,`2. The machine rhyme: the 1940-1971 object, machine or trade that shares that action's shape, rhythm or feeling. Be specific and concrete.`,`3. The tender thing: the affectionate human truth underneath the product. This is what stops the look being decorative. Find the care, the inheritance, the promise kept, the person who did something so someone else would be alright.`,`4. The kept motif: one single visual element, drawn from the machine rhyme, that every concept in this shoot will carry.`,`Then name the place. It must be a REAL place, named as it was named in the period: where the product's main ingredient is grown or traded, where its trade or craft was practised, where its ritual belongs, or where its category has genuine history. Prefer a real Indian place a viewer would recognise, using its period name where it had one, such as Bombay, Madras, Calcutta, Poona, Mysore, Cawnpore, Darjeeling or Malabar, unless the product's genuine history plainly belongs somewhere else. Never invent a fictional sector, zone, station or valley. A viewer who can look the place up gets meaning; an invented one is just set dressing. Give the reason it is the right origin.`,`Then translate the brand's colour into period ink. 1960s offset lithography could not reach modern packaging saturation, so state the muted, slightly faded equivalent the era would actually have printed. Every card uses this shifted value, not the original.`,`Then restyle the packaging: describe how this exact product would have been manufactured and packaged in 1971, using period materials and closures. Keep the brand's silhouette recognisable in spirit but change the format to what the period would actually have used.`,`The logo is the exception. Describe the period packaging as carrying the brand's existing logo unchanged, in its existing letterforms and arrangement, printed in period ink. Never describe a redrawn, re-lettered, engraved, scripted or otherwise period-styled version of it. Materials, format, closure and colour move to 1971; the logo does not.`,`One of the label strings must be the brand name spelled exactly as it appears on the real packaging, with the same capitalisation.`,`Then find the events. Give between two and six period occasions a viewer of this product would find relatable, each one connected to this product's own category, ingredient, ritual, trade or industry. The connection is the whole point: the occasion must be one this product would genuinely have been part of, or one that shares its action, its season or its reason for existing. An occasion beats a mood, because people recognise an event immediately.`,`The audience is Indian, so the occasions must be ones an Indian viewer recognises from their own family memory: harvest and festival days, a mill or cooperative open day, a wedding season, the first rain of the monsoon, a school prize day, a village fair, a new railway line or factory opening, a Republic Day parade. Avoid occasions that only read in a European or American context.`,`Mark an event 'documented' only if it genuinely happened and you are confident of the name and period. Anything you are inventing or generalising, a harvest fair, a works open day, a first day of term, a season opener, is 'period-typical' and must be described generically without a name or a date, because printed artwork that names an event is making a claim of record.`,`Prefer occasions with strong visual hooks: crowds, bunting, trestle tables, a ribbon, a scoreboard, a queue, a marquee, a procession.`].join(`\n`),packshotInstruction:[`Render one canonical packshot of the restyled packaging on a plain empty warm bone background, centred and upright, printed face square to camera, flat even frontal light with a soft contact shadow, the whole container visible with generous margin.`,`No props, no scene, no horizon line, no halftone, no film grain, no vignette. This asset is a reference for later stages and must be clean and legible.`,`Hold the original packaging's proportions. Match the reference product's aspect ratio and relative dimensions, and do not stretch, elongate, squash or otherwise reshape it while restyling. Every later shot inherits these proportions, so distortion here propagates through the whole shoot.`].join(`\n`),logoIntegrity:[`The brand's logo is the single element that does not get restyled. Reproduce it from the reference images exactly as it is drawn there.`,`Hold its structure: the same letterforms and typeface, the same spelling and capitalisation, the same letter spacing and word breaks, the same symbol or emblem drawn the same way, the same arrangement of mark to wordmark, the same internal proportions, and the same relative size and position on the packaging.`,`Do not redraw it in a period typeface, do not convert it to an engraving, a monogram, a script or a serif version, do not simplify or stylise the symbol, do not respace or re-letter the wordmark, do not add a frame, banner, crest, ribbon or roundel around it, and do not translate or abbreviate it.`,`Only its printing may change: the ink colour shifts to the period ink, and it receives the same reduced saturation, ink density, softness at the edges and paper texture as everything else on the sheet. It is the same logo, printed by an older press.`].join(` `),outputImageSize:Qa,archetypes:[{id:`multi-view-grid`,name:`Multi-view souvenir grid`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`Four photographic panels in a two by two grid separated by aged cream gutters, each panel a different view of the world, an empty caption strip under each, and a coloured band across the top carrying the place name.`},{id:`linen-vista`,name:`Hand-tinted linen vista`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A scenic view on heavy linen weave stock composed for a tall frame, deep foreground leading to a high horizon, monochrome photographic base with flat oversaturated hand-applied colour slightly off register, the brand present only as a small weathered roadside sign. The product itself does not appear.`},{id:`hand-tinted-portrait`,name:`Hand-tinted portrait card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A single person photographed in a quiet unguarded moment, monochrome base with soft translucent hand colouring on the skin, the product small and incidental in the frame, a short script caption in a corner.`},{id:`tall-tale-exaggeration`,name:`Tall-tale exaggeration card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`The product enlarged to the scale of a building and placed in a real landscape as a roadside monument, with tiny people at its base for scale, in the cheerful absurd tradition of 'world's largest' souvenir cards.`},{id:`travel-poster`,name:`Travel bureau poster card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A flat screen-printed travel poster in three or four colours, bold simplified shapes, a strong diagonal or horizon, generous empty bands top and bottom reserved for typesetting.`},{id:`aerial-view`,name:`Aerial survey card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A high oblique aerial photograph of the place, roads and fields and works laid out below, in the manner of a mid-century aerial survey postcard, with a small caption in one corner.`},{id:`night-view`,name:`Night view card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`The same world after dark, lit windows and lamps and signage glowing warm against a deep blue-black, printed with heavy ink and visible halftone in the shadows, in the tradition of 'by night' souvenir cards.`},{id:`main-street-view`,name:`Main street view`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A street-level view down a working high street or works road, shopfronts and signage receding, ordinary people going about their day, the brand appearing naturally as painted signage.`},{id:`real-photo-rppc`,name:`Real photo card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A plain silver-gelatin real photo postcard with a white border and no printing at all, the kind an individual had made from their own negative. Documentary, slightly awkward framing, entirely unstyled.`},{id:`advertising-trade-card`,name:`Advertising trade card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A chromolithographed advertising trade card with a decorative border, a small illustrated vignette, and the product presented as a proud manufacturer's announcement.`},{id:`panoramic-foldout`,name:`Concertina fold-out`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A tall view unfolded from a souvenir concertina booklet, with two visible horizontal fold creases across it and slight colour misalignment along each crease, the scene continuing past them.`},{id:`weather-report-card`,name:`Weather report card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A novelty card built around a printed instrument reading: a dial, chart recorder trace, gauge or barometer face dominating the composition, with the world visible behind it.`},{id:`comic-cartoon-card`,name:`Comic cartoon card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A flat two-colour cartoon drawing with bold ink outlines and simple fills, gently funny, in the seaside comic postcard tradition, with an empty band left for a caption.`},{id:`works-tour-card`,name:`Works tour card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`An industrial interior shown with civic pride: the hall, the line, the machinery, workers at their stations, in the tradition of cards a factory printed for visitors on a works tour.`},{id:`holiday-greeting-card`,name:`Seasonal greeting card`,orientation:`portrait`,brief:`A warm seasonal greeting card with a decorative border, a small central scene, and generous space for a handwritten message, printed in a limited festive ink set.`}],guaranteedArchetypeIds:[`real-photo-rppc`],worlds:[{id:`mid-century-india`,name:`India, 1940 to 1971`,isDefault:!0,brief:`The look's native world: the first decades after independence. Textile mills and cooperative dairies, steel plants and canal works, tea gardens and paddy fields, railway platforms with enamel station boards, brass and copper vessels, hand pumps and bullock carts beside new tractors, Ambassador cars and scooters, transistor radios, ceiling fans in tiled offices, printing presses, school compounds, ordinary people at ordinary work. Warm dusty light, monsoon greys, whitewash and ochre walls.`,printFraming:`A souvenir, advertising or documentary card printed in the same era it depicts.`,productTreatment:`The packaging sits naturally in the scene as a contemporary object, on a shop counter, a kitchen shelf, a windowsill, a steel trunk or a wooden bench.`,occasions:[]},{id:`mughal-india`,name:`Mughal India, seen through 1960s Indian print`,isDefault:!1,maxConcepts:2,share:.15,brief:`Daily life in Mughal India as 1960s Indian calendar art and film posters staged it: sandstone and marble courtyards, scalloped arches and jali screens, char bagh gardens with water channels, bazaar stalls under cloth awnings, carpets and low cushions, brass trays and long-necked surahis, figures in muslin and block-printed cotton, mango and neem trees. Populated and ordinary rather than imperial. Warm afternoon light, painted backdrops, the slightly stagey richness of a studio historical rather than an archaeological reconstruction.`,printFraming:`A 1960s printed artifact ABOUT the Mughal period, never a modern photograph of it: a hand-painted film poster, a calendar art print of the kind given away by a mill or a general store, an illustrated history card slipped into a packet of biscuits, or a tourist card of the monuments. The halftone, faded ink, paper grain and edge wear of the look are unchanged.`,productTreatment:`The locked packaging appears plainly inside the scene as an object in use or on display: on a stone ledge, among bazaar goods, on a carpet beside a tray, held by a figure, or presented on a museum plinth as a find. Never redesign it into a period vessel and never hide it. The pleasure is that it is simply, matter-of-factly there.`,occasions:[`Diwali lamps being set along a courtyard wall`,`a wedding procession moving through the bazaar`,`market day at the chowk`,`the first rain of the monsoon breaking over the gardens`,`a harvest being weighed and loaded`,`a royal kitchen preparing for a feast`]},{id:`classical-india`,name:`Classical India, seen through 1960s Indian print`,isDefault:!1,maxConcepts:2,share:.15,brief:`Daily life in classical and early medieval India as 1960s Indian print imagined it: granite temple courtyards and stepped tanks, carved pillars and stone elephants, village streets of thatch and mud walls, potters and weavers and oil presses at work, bullock carts on cart tracks, banana groves, coconut palms and banyan shade, brass lamps and terracotta pots, figures in unstitched cotton. Ordinary and lived-in rather than monumental. Strong south Indian light, deep shade, painted backdrops.`,printFraming:`A 1960s printed artifact ABOUT the classical past, never a modern photograph of it: a mythological calendar print, a school history chart, an illustrated card given away with matchboxes or sweets, or a temple tourist card. The halftone, faded ink, paper grain and edge wear of the look are unchanged.`,productTreatment:`The locked packaging appears plainly inside the scene as an object in use or on display: on a temple ledge, among market goods, beside a potter's wheel, held by a figure, or presented on a plinth as a museum find. Never redesign it into a period vessel and never hide it. The pleasure is that it is simply, matter-of-factly there.`,occasions:[`a temple car being pulled through the street`,`the harvest festival with cattle painted and garlanded`,`market day beside the stepped tank`,`the first rain breaking over the fields`,`an oil press working through the evening`,`a village fair with drummers and stalls`]}]};function ro(e){let t=e.trim(),n=t.split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean).length,r=t.length;return{wordCount:n,characterCount:r,fitsPrintedCard:n>=26&&n<=40&&r<=240}}function io(e){return typeof e==`string`&&e.trim().length>0?e.trim():null}function ao(e){return e&&typeof e==`object`&&!Array.isArray(e)?e:null}function oo(e){let t=ao(e.generativeLookConcept);if(!t)return null;let n=io(t.backText);if(!n)return null;let r=ao(e.generativeLookPlace);return{backText:n,...ro(n),title:io(t.title),premise:io(t.premise),even