ew where those details would not be visible, omit them instead of moving them onto the wrong face.\\n\\nThe attached product photos show the real product — reproduce it faithfully.\\n\\nThe final attached image is the official brand logo. Add it once, subtly, in clean negative space; keep it exact, legible, and uncropped.\\n\\nDesign requirements (apply to this ad):\\n- Follow the instructions by the director.\\n- Make the ad design, layout, typography on-branded, but should look premium, editorial-like.\\n- Establish a clear visual hierarchy: one dominant focal point the eye lands on\\n  first, then the supporting message, then the CTA.\\n- Keep strong figure/ground contrast. If text sits over imagery, place a scrim or\\n  overlay behind it so it stays fully legible.\\n- Place the focal subject on a rule-of-thirds line; leave generous whitespace and\\n  avoid clutter.\\n- Use a limited palette anchored to the brand colors (~60-30-10), reserving one\\n  accent color for the call to action.\\n- Keep 20% space all around the creative for the ad to look cleaner\\n- Group and align elements cleanly (Gestalt); use at most two type styles.\",\"status\":\"generating\",\"createdBy\":\"f5dfb377-dac7-46a6-aea3-a4e04cb45e54\",\"metadata\":{\"brief\":\"Based on the Problem-Agitate-Solve (PAS) framework, this static 1:1 ad targets consumers who love soda but hate the bloating and digestive discomfort that traditional sugary drinks cause. The layout is an elegant, in-context lifestyle photograph set in a bright, modern kitchen during a sunny morning. The visual hero is the outcome: a cold, condensation-ringed pastel lavender OLIPOP Classic Grape can next to a beautiful tall glass filled with ice, dark purple grape soda, and a fresh lime wedge, symbolizing a clean, refreshing, and gut-friendly afternoon treat. Text is kept minimal, under 20% of the frame. The on-image headline reads \\\"The soda that loves your gut.\\\" set in WindsorEF in Deep Forest. A simple checkmark icon poin