e bottom. The upper shoulder of the can features the phrase Supports Digestive Health in small dark forest-green sans-serif text. Flanking the central graphics on the left are the words Prebiotics, Botanicals, and Plant Fiber, with Non GMO at the bottom left; the right side features a small circular Plant Powered Microbiome Approved seal and the callout 5g of sugar per can at the bottom right. A secondary version of the product is also visible, which reverses the colorway to feature a deep royal-purple can body with white lettering for the brand name, flavor, and side text, while the grape graphic is highlighted with lighter lavender tones.\\n\\nUse the product appearance text only as a private visual reference for accurately drawing the product. Do not print, quote, paraphrase, label, or turn any of that description into ad copy. Treat orientation-specific details carefully: show front-only details only when the product front is visible; if the brief shows the back, side, top, or an angled view where those details would not be visible, omit them instead of moving them onto the wrong face.\\n\\nThe attached product photos show the real product — reproduce it faithfully.\\n\\nThe final attached image is the official brand logo. Add it once, subtly, in clean negative space; keep it exact, legible, and uncropped.\\n\\nDesign requirements (apply to this ad):\\n- Follow the instructions by the director.\\n- Make the ad design, layout, typography on-branded, but should look premium, editorial-like.\\n- Establish a clear visual hierarchy: one dominant focal point the eye lands on\\n  first, then the supporting message, then the CTA.\\n- Keep strong figure/ground contrast. If text sits over imagery, place a scrim or\\n  overlay behind it so it stays fully legible.\\n- Place the focal subject on a rule-of-thirds line; leave generous whitespace and\\n  avoid clutter.\\n- Use a limited palette anchored to the brand colors (~60-30-10), reserving one\\n  accent color for the call to action.\\n- Keep 20% space all around the creative for the ad to look cleaner\\n- Group and align elements cleanly (Gestalt); use at most two type styles.\",\"status\":\"generated\",\"createdBy\":\"f5dfb377-dac7-46a6-aea3-a4e04cb45e54\",\"metadata\":{\"brief\":\"Based on the Side-by-Side Comparison (SBSC) framework, this static 1:1 graphic directly compares OLIPOP Classic Grape to traditional grape soda to prove it is the ultimate health upgrade. The layout is a clean, split-screen design. The left half has a charcoal background showing a generic, dusty-looking purple soda can dripping with artificial syrup next to a harsh red warning label reading \\\"39g Sugar\\\". The right half features a warm, welcoming off-white background highlighting the matte, pastel lavender-lilac OLIPOP Classic Grape can sitting on a clean surface with a small checkmark list pointing to \\\"5g Sugar\\\" and \\\"9g Fiber\\\". The visual hero is the contrast between the toxic old choice and the clean, premium new upgrade. The on-image headline is \\\"Same nostalgic taste, 85% less sugar.\\\" in the bold, vintage WindsorEF typeface in Hunter Green, keeping text under 20% of the image. The single call to action is a pill-shaped Citron Yellow button that reads \\\"Compare Now\\\". To keep this ad on-brand, use WindsorEF for the main headline, use Hunter Green for the right-side elements, ensure the off-white background feels creamy and organic, and avoid pure black in favor of Charcoal for secondary text.\",\"prompt\":\"Instructions by the director: Based on the Side-by-Side Comparison (SBSC) framework, this static 1:1 graphic directly compares OLIPOP Classic Grape to traditional grape soda to prove it is the ultimate health upgrade. The layout is a clean, split-screen design. The left half has a charcoal background showing a generic, dusty-looking purple soda can dripping with artificial syrup next to a harsh red warning label reading \\\"39g Sugar\\\". The right half features a warm, we